WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.880
Space Telescope is in a way

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.730
a little like Galileo's first telescope.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:11.310
Wherever Galileo pointed his telescope,

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.410
he made major new discoveries.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:15.840
Look at the Moon, you find
mountains and craters,

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.640
look at Saturn, you find rings,

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.590
look at the Milky Way and you find

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:23.193
that it is littered and composed of stars.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.150
If you could look back in time,

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:31.550
when would you look back to?

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:36.510
Early man?

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:37.983
The time of the dinosaurs?

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.180
Or the birth of our planet?

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.360
We like to say telescopes
are like time machines.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.640
And of course the reason
that this is possible

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.100
is because light takes time to travel.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:07.500
So the street lamp across the street

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.780
takes a teeny tiny fraction of a second

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.550
for the light to reach your eye.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.800
The light from the sun
takes about eight minutes

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:14.820
to reach the earth.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.500
So in essence, we're seeing the sun

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:17.913
as it was eight minutes ago.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:21.510
Now, a new telescope

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:23.100
that's more powerful than anything

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.320
that's ever been sent
into space is looking

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:28.410
at some of the farthest
and oldest objects yet.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:32.850
It's called the James Webb
Space Telescope or JWST,

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.910
and it can see back to the
beginning of the Universe

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:37.950
when the first galaxies started to form

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:40.083
and emit light after the Big Bang.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.370
One of the great things
about telescopes like this

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.340
is they're really designed
to see the entire Universe.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:52.110
So all the way to 13 and a half
billion years into the past,

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:55.590
all the way to our cosmic
backyard of the solar system,

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:57.933
and everything in space
and time in between.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:02.220
Finally, after 30 years in the making,

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:06.900
a successful launch into space,
50 successful deployments,

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.390
1 million miles traveled from Earth,

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:13.650
months of cooling to near
absolute zero and calibration,

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.900
it has delivered its first, full color,

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:17.373
science-quality images.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:32.700
These images showcase the
telescope's powerful instruments

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:35.403
and offer a preview of the
science projects to come.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.070
These first images really do signify

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:43.680
the start of science operations.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.280
We have over a year of
observations already planned,

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.150
some really exciting things
that we already have designed

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:53.493
to do in the first year of operations.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.970
The hope of scientists is to uncover

00:02:56.970 --> 00:03:00.120
the greatest mysteries
of the universe today.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:04.380
We would like to observe
galaxies as far away as possible.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:07.230
Red giant stars in the disc of Andromeda.

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:10.170
Star-forming regions,
one is in the Milky way,

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:13.920
our galaxy and two nearby
galaxies, the Magellanic clouds.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:15.480
The Trappist-1 system,

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.820
what we can call the
rockstar of exoplanets.

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.340
TNOs, these are objects that are orbiting

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:22.020
beyond the orbit of Neptune.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:25.380
So in the coldest regions
of our solar system.

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:29.310
How stars, galaxies,
planets, et cetera formed.

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:32.223
And that really helps us
understand how we fit in.

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:38.400
So we have all these
very specific questions

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:40.830
that we've planned to try to answer.

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:43.140
And I think we'll for sure answer those,

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:45.660
but I think we'll also learn things

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:47.493
that we haven't even dreamed of yet.

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:00.167
Lift off with the Space Shuttle Discovery

00:04:00.167 --> 00:04:03.890
with the Hubble Space Telescope,
our window on the Universe.

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:06.600
In 1990, The Hubble Space Telescope

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.633
successfully launched aboard
the Space Shuttle Discovery.

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:13.860
It's been serviced by
humans in space five times,

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:15.960
allowing it to be one
of the longest running,

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.423
and most expensive
science missions to date.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:25.410
For more than 30 years, through
its powerful imaging system,

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:28.560
it's changed our
understanding of the universe.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:33.420
Galaxies, nebulae and star clusters

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:35.493
have been seen like never before.

00:04:36.778 --> 00:04:38.910
The Hubble Space Telescope
grew from the idea

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:41.730
that by putting an observatory into space

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:44.880
above the atmosphere, you
could get much sharper pictures

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:47.913
without that blurring that
the Earth's atmosphere brings.

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:51.540
So looking at places in our own Milky Way,

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:53.310
where stars are being born,

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:54.810
and we are beginning to see evidence

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:58.440
for planets being born in discs of gas

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:01.260
and dust swirling around those stars.

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:03.600
Things that weren't even
thought of at the time

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.930
when Hubble was built, include
measuring the atmospheres

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:09.960
of planets going around other stars.

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:11.700
And then beyond that of course,

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:15.000
the very big picture has
been looking at galaxies

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.603
all the way back close to the
beginning of the Universe.

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:24.030
Perhaps one of the most
well-known images came

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:26.403
from what's known as
the Hubble Deep Field.

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:31.590
Instead of looking at
previously-known galaxies observed

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:33.540
from ground-based telescopes,

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:36.960
they pointed Hubble at a
relatively empty part of the sky

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:40.590
and took hundreds of long
exposures over a 10-day period,

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:41.853
then combined them.

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:48.663
This first image changed the
way humanity saw the universe.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:55.110
Almost all of the roughly
3000 objects captured here

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:57.333
is a previously unknown galaxy.

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:02.070
So Hubble has actually helped
us illustrate the evolution

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:06.330
of the Universe from the
first cold gas and dust,

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:10.530
which was lying around millions
of years after the Big Bang

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:13.080
and how that coalesced and agglomerated

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:17.280
and swirled together to form
galaxies full of shining stars.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:19.770
So that's one of the
great successes of Hubble.

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:21.420
So to follow on from that science

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:24.030
and see the birth of the first galaxies,

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:27.210
we need a bigger telescope
and we need one that operates

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:30.663
in the infrared and that's the
James Webb Space Telescope.

00:06:34.788 --> 00:06:38.100
JWST is named after James E. Webb,

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:39.990
NASA's second administrator

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:42.420
who oversaw the agency's
decade-long effort

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:43.980
to reach the moon.

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:46.770
He believed NASA had
to put as much emphasis

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:49.743
on pushing space science
as human space flight.

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:54.360
This next generation telescope
is not only 100 times

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:56.550
more powerful than Hubble but its ability

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:59.943
to see infrared opens up
a world of opportunities.

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:04.530
It means that scientists are
now able to detect frequencies

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:07.290
of light that are invisible to Hubble.

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:09.570
The Hubble space telescope
operates primarily

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:10.410
in the optical.

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:13.230
So the wavelengths that you and I can see.

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:16.650
But as we look at the most
distant galaxies in the Universe,

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:18.630
because the Universe is expanding,

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:22.500
those galaxies are in essence,
receding from us at faster

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:24.030
and faster speeds.

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:27.780
And that means that their light
is shifted from the visible

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:29.580
into the near infrared.

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:30.810
So in fact, when we want to see

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:32.640
the very most distant galaxies,

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:35.700
the first ones which ever
formed in the Universe,

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:37.170
Hubble can't see them.

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:40.080
It's unable to see at
the right wavelengths.

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:41.760
And also it's not really big enough

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:44.820
to see those extremely faint objects.

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:47.880
This shift in wavelengths
is known as redshift

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:50.130
and the result is that
the most distant objects

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:52.290
emitting visible light are invisible

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:54.933
to telescopes tuned to
optical wavelengths.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:59.100
So you need a big telescope
operating in the infrared

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:03.150
like JWST in order to see
those very first galaxies,

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:06.540
the ones which are moving
so fast away from us,

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:09.843
that all of their light is
redshifted into the infrared.

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:14.790
Now, JWST's largest program

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:18.300
during its first cycle is
going to put those capabilities

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:19.233
to the test.

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:32.580
The COSMOS-Webb survey is going

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:36.750
to be looking 13.5 billion
years into the past,

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:40.383
about 200 to 300 million
years after the Big Bang.

00:08:43.818 --> 00:08:46.860
And what it's going to be
able to do is observe galaxies

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:49.200
that are much further away from us

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:51.090
than we've been able to do before.

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:52.650
So we're going to be able to find

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:54.840
some of the earliest galaxies

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:56.703
that have formed in the Universe.

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:02.367
The Hubble deep field looked
at one part of the sky

00:09:02.367 --> 00:09:04.260
and stared there for a long time.

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:06.180
And that was really our first look

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:08.373
into the distant universe.

00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:16.770
With COSMOS-Web, we are going
to go a big step beyond that,

00:09:16.770 --> 00:09:19.980
so not only will we be
able to look at objects

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:21.480
that are much farther away from us

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:23.910
and much deeper into
the Universe's history.

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:26.970
We're going to cover a much
larger area of the sky.

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:29.790
So we're going to be able to observe many,

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:33.150
many thousands more galaxies
than we're able to be seen

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:35.300
in the small area of
the Hubble Deep Field.

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:45.480
So right now with Hubble

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:48.270
at these early Universe time periods,

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:49.470
you don't see very many.

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:50.910
And what you see is a smudge,

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:52.290
just a little bit of a blob.

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:55.170
With JWST, we'll be able
to resolve structure.

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:57.780
We're gonna learn a lot
about the properties

00:09:57.780 --> 00:09:59.550
of our very early Universe.

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:03.480
How the earliest structures
first started to grow.

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:05.700
But JWST will also tell us a lot

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:07.800
about how those galaxies evolved

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:10.440
from the very early Universe until today,

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:13.800
because we'll be able to see
galaxies across a wide range

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:15.630
of distances all the way back.

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:19.260
So we can put together a
timeline of how galaxies evolve

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:21.873
and how their structures
have grown overall.

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.940
The images are going to
have a very high resolution,

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:32.340
which means we can resolve
a lot of structures

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:35.520
and see really fine details
that we can't do with Hubble.

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:38.430
So I fully expect some of
the initial images we see

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:40.770
are going to be jaw dropping.

00:10:40.770 --> 00:10:42.480
And so overall, we estimate

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:46.110
that we're going to detect
several thousand galaxies

00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:47.820
in the very early Universe,

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:50.220
but how many we actually detect tells us

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:52.320
something very important
about the physics.

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:54.780
So even if we detect less than we expect,

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:56.310
that's still really interesting

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:57.910
from a scientific point of view.

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:04.950
If you had spoken to people
that were expecting observations

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:07.410
from the first Hubble data,
when Hubble first launched

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:09.780
and told them about all
the amazing discoveries

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:10.740
Hubble has made since then,

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:12.270
it would probably blow their mind, right?

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:14.940
They never would've
imagined we'd see galaxies

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:17.790
as far away as we do or that we'd observe,

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:19.950
planets around their stars, et cetera.

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:22.710
So we fully expect James
Webb Space Telescope

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:25.593
will find things we never
even knew to ask about.

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:31.290
Unlike JWST,

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:34.260
we can't see infrared
light with the human eye,

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:36.240
but we can detect it through heat

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:39.930
or experience it visually
using an infrared camera.

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:42.960
Infrared light, you can think
of it like heat radiation.

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:45.060
So everything glows in infrared,

00:11:45.060 --> 00:11:47.400
you and I are glowing and infrared.

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:48.780
A lot of things in space

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:50.820
give off infrared light too,

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:54.063
like stars, nebulae, and galaxies.

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:56.250
But to detect the faint,

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:58.800
far away targets scientists are after,

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:02.553
JWST has to be away from
the Earth's warm atmosphere.

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:08.640
The problem is with Hubble

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:10.500
is that Hubble is actually quite warm.

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:12.960
Hubble is in low earth orbit,

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:15.300
and it's about the same
temperature as the earth.

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:18.810
And that means it's actually
emitting in the infrared.

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:19.643
And that's a bad thing

00:12:19.643 --> 00:12:21.780
if you want to detect faint infrared light

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:23.700
from very far away,

00:12:23.700 --> 00:12:25.890
because you are swamped
by the light coming

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:27.930
from the telescope itself.

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:31.350
So the James Webb Space
telescope has to be cold,

00:12:31.350 --> 00:12:36.350
really cold, minus 233
degrees Celsius cold.

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:39.480
So the way we achieve that is firstly,

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:41.220
by making the telescope open,

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:43.080
it doesn't have a tube around it.

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:46.080
But what it does have is a huge sunshield,

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:49.470
and the telescope sits
behind that sunshield.

00:12:49.470 --> 00:12:53.100
We're also located at a place called L2,

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:56.250
1.5 million kilometers away from the earth

00:12:56.250 --> 00:12:59.220
where the sun and the earth
are always on the same side

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:00.153
of the telescope.

00:13:01.230 --> 00:13:02.580
In this location,

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:04.890
the telescope's main instruments can cool

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:07.140
to near absolute zero temperatures

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:08.973
and receive the infrared light.

00:13:10.410 --> 00:13:13.440
Then, they can take that
light and produce images,

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:17.310
or analyze the light to learn
what materials are present.

00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:19.470
This is known as spectroscopy

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:23.163
and is a major feature of
JWST's science instruments.

00:13:24.630 --> 00:13:27.210
We have four instruments on the telescope.

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:29.850
And these different
instruments are optimized

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:31.500
to do different things.

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:34.200
So obviously, the imagers take images

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:36.480
of the Universe in both the near-infrared

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:39.810
and the mid-infrared part of the spectrum.

00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:42.000
The spectrographs take spectra

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:43.650
of different objects in the Universe.

00:13:43.650 --> 00:13:47.130
So spectra are like you can
think of as like fingerprints

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:47.963
of the objects.

00:13:47.963 --> 00:13:51.420
So spectra help tell us about
the chemical components,

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:53.340
and what atoms and molecules

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:56.610
are in the various different
things that we're looking at.

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:58.500
Using two of these instruments,

00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:01.770
NIRCam and NIRspec, and
the power of the telescope,

00:14:01.770 --> 00:14:04.500
one team is going to
be able to take images

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:07.080
and reveal the composition
of individual stars

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:11.073
in our nearest large galaxy,
the Andromeda Galaxy.

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:29.373
So we'll be looking 2.5
million years into the past.

00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:34.260
That means that the light
that we're seeing was emitted

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:36.603
by the galaxy 2.5 million years ago.

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:40.830
Andromeda is the nearest
large galaxy to the Milky way.

00:14:40.830 --> 00:14:42.210
It's our twin, if you like.

00:14:42.210 --> 00:14:44.490
They're both disc galaxies
and it's actually moving

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:45.630
towards the Milky Way as well.

00:14:45.630 --> 00:14:48.150
They're gravitationally pulling each other

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:51.303
towards one another and
eventually will actually collide.

00:14:55.290 --> 00:14:57.360
We see the Milky Way from inside,

00:14:57.360 --> 00:14:59.970
and that means we can just
see the individual stars

00:14:59.970 --> 00:15:00.960
in the sky.

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:03.210
When we look at galaxies
outside the Milky Way,

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:04.320
we just see them as a whole

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:06.990
and you can't really pick
out individual stars.

00:15:06.990 --> 00:15:09.210
The great thing about Andromeda
is we're still looking at it

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:10.440
from the outside in,

00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:12.570
but we can actually pick
out individual stars

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:13.920
in the disc of Andromeda.

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:17.610
Which makes it a fantastic
tool for doing this comparison

00:15:17.610 --> 00:15:19.140
where you want to extend the science

00:15:19.140 --> 00:15:20.190
that we're doing in the Milky Way

00:15:20.190 --> 00:15:23.163
to this whole galactic population.

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:28.230
Because we study the Milky
Way in so much detail,

00:15:28.230 --> 00:15:30.903
it's like the Rosetta
Stone of galaxy formation,

00:15:30.903 --> 00:15:33.990
that we can use to inform our
understanding of galaxies.

00:15:33.990 --> 00:15:36.660
But of course then if the
Milky Way is an outlier

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:39.540
and galaxies, like Andromeda
look much more run of the mill.

00:15:39.540 --> 00:15:40.800
Then that means a lot of the things

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:43.283
that we know about the
Milky Way are not so useful.

00:15:44.580 --> 00:15:47.250
Other telescopes, while
there's plenty of telescopes

00:15:47.250 --> 00:15:48.300
that are good.

00:15:48.300 --> 00:15:51.570
They're just not quite good
enough to do this task.

00:15:51.570 --> 00:15:55.050
So we're gonna take spectra of 300 stars

00:15:55.050 --> 00:15:56.880
in the disc of Andromeda.

00:15:56.880 --> 00:15:59.340
And so what that means is
by measuring the regions

00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:01.860
of those spectra, where we see less light,

00:16:01.860 --> 00:16:03.990
we can actually understand
the element abundances,

00:16:03.990 --> 00:16:06.630
like oxygen, magnesium and silicon.

00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:09.960
Before we take those spectra,
we're gonna use the NIRCam,

00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:12.420
which is the near-infrared camera.

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:14.580
And we're gonna essentially
use that to take images

00:16:14.580 --> 00:16:17.130
of Andromeda before we
go and take these spectra

00:16:17.130 --> 00:16:19.410
to make sure that we can
really target the regions

00:16:19.410 --> 00:16:21.033
of the galaxy that we need to.

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:26.610
What I'm interested in
doing is going and checking

00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:29.850
whether this characteristic
element abundance distribution

00:16:29.850 --> 00:16:32.490
that we see in the Milky
Way is present in Andromeda,

00:16:32.490 --> 00:16:33.930
which is something that
we've not been able to do

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:35.073
without JWST.

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:40.650
We know that all the galaxies
we see in the Universe

00:16:40.650 --> 00:16:43.320
have been forming
throughout the whole history

00:16:43.320 --> 00:16:44.153
of our universe.

00:16:44.153 --> 00:16:47.730
So galaxies that we
see are a fossil record

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:49.650
of the whole of galaxy formation.

00:16:49.650 --> 00:16:51.450
If you could link all of the dots together

00:16:51.450 --> 00:16:53.820
and you understood galaxy
formation very well,

00:16:53.820 --> 00:16:56.400
you could actually turn back
the clock on all the physics

00:16:56.400 --> 00:16:57.600
that happened in those galaxies

00:16:57.600 --> 00:16:59.850
and get to something like
the initial conditions

00:16:59.850 --> 00:17:02.070
of our universe where all
of everything came from.

00:17:02.070 --> 00:17:04.050
And I think those things
are just interesting

00:17:04.050 --> 00:17:06.630
and a project like this is telling us

00:17:06.630 --> 00:17:09.750
about the origins of our own galaxy.

00:17:09.750 --> 00:17:11.900
Most of us want to know
where we came from.

00:17:13.950 --> 00:17:16.350
Building a telescope that has enough power

00:17:16.350 --> 00:17:19.350
and sophistication to enable
these sorts of projects

00:17:19.350 --> 00:17:21.120
wasn't straightforward.

00:17:21.120 --> 00:17:23.850
Engineers had to come up
with a completely new design

00:17:23.850 --> 00:17:25.263
in comparison to Hubble.

00:17:30.330 --> 00:17:33.300
So this telescope has this giant mirror,

00:17:33.300 --> 00:17:36.720
about 6.5 meters across, about 22 feet.

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:39.900
And it has a sunshield that's
the size of a tennis court.

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:43.950
And so it's an odd looking
design, an odd looking telescope.

00:17:43.950 --> 00:17:47.490
The reason it was designed this
way is because it's so big.

00:17:47.490 --> 00:17:49.500
It stands about four stories tall

00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:52.320
so it's too big for any rocket

00:17:52.320 --> 00:17:54.570
to fit inside all unfolded in a rocket.

00:17:54.570 --> 00:17:56.880
So we had to fold it up
to fit inside the rocket

00:17:56.880 --> 00:17:59.133
and it unfolded once it was in space.

00:18:01.140 --> 00:18:03.120
Since space bound rockets do fail

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:08.120
from time to time, over three
decades of work, $10 billion,

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:11.553
and thousands of scientists'
work were on the line.

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:18.130
And we have engine start.

00:18:20.865 --> 00:18:21.865
And liftoff.

00:18:23.173 --> 00:18:26.220
Decollage, liftoff, from
a tropical rainforest

00:18:26.220 --> 00:18:28.290
to the edge of time itself,

00:18:28.290 --> 00:18:30.960
James Webb begins a
voyage back to the birth

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:32.670
of the Universe.

00:18:32.670 --> 00:18:35.880
Honestly, the launch was
definitely not the hardest part

00:18:35.880 --> 00:18:36.783
of this mission.

00:18:38.760 --> 00:18:43.200
So following launch, we had
two weeks of really intense,

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:46.650
complex, complicated deployments

00:18:46.650 --> 00:18:49.080
to get this telescope unfolded.

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:52.320
And myself and I think
all of us on the team

00:18:52.320 --> 00:18:54.120
were really on the edge of our seats

00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:56.613
to wait for these deployments to happen.

00:18:58.200 --> 00:18:59.970
Given its distance to the Earth,

00:18:59.970 --> 00:19:03.090
if something went wrong
with JWST's deployments,

00:19:03.090 --> 00:19:04.440
there wouldn't be repair missions

00:19:04.440 --> 00:19:05.807
like there was with Hubble.

00:19:07.350 --> 00:19:12.000
The deployment of the sunshield
was particularly intense.

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:15.540
The sunshield itself is five
layers and it had to unfold

00:19:15.540 --> 00:19:18.090
and then deploy the five layers.

00:19:18.090 --> 00:19:21.330
And there were hundreds
of release mechanisms

00:19:21.330 --> 00:19:22.260
on the sunshield

00:19:22.260 --> 00:19:25.260
that all had to release
at just the right time

00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:26.820
in just the right way.

00:19:26.820 --> 00:19:29.580
There were quite a few
single point failures

00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:32.730
so that if one release
mechanism didn't work,

00:19:32.730 --> 00:19:34.440
there would be nothing
we could do to fix it.

00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:37.380
And so this particular
part of the deployments,

00:19:37.380 --> 00:19:41.400
watching the sunshield deploy
was incredibly stressful.

00:19:41.400 --> 00:19:43.950
But everything went off so well.

00:19:43.950 --> 00:19:47.790
The engineers have built an
amazing, incredible telescope.

00:19:47.790 --> 00:19:49.950
And after that two weeks,

00:19:49.950 --> 00:19:51.480
I think all of us took a deep breath.

00:19:51.480 --> 00:19:53.280
There was definitely a big celebration

00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:56.223
after the last part of
the deployments happened.

00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:03.300
The launch was so incredibly efficient,

00:20:03.300 --> 00:20:05.490
using less fuel than anticipated,

00:20:05.490 --> 00:20:07.950
it extended the mission from five years,

00:20:07.950 --> 00:20:10.263
to an estimated 10 to 15 years.

00:20:13.170 --> 00:20:15.930
Months later, after the
instruments had cooled,

00:20:15.930 --> 00:20:17.700
there were a series of calibrations

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:19.623
to make sure everything was aligned.

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:25.680
The primary mirror is made
up of 18 different segments.

00:20:25.680 --> 00:20:29.370
And those segments had
to be perfectly aligned

00:20:29.370 --> 00:20:31.170
once the telescope was in space.

00:20:31.170 --> 00:20:35.280
So what we did was point the
telescope at a bright star,

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:37.710
and then you essentially get back an image

00:20:37.710 --> 00:20:39.210
of 18 different stars.

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:43.530
And so from using that image
and tracing the star image

00:20:43.530 --> 00:20:46.860
to the different segments, we
were able to individually move

00:20:46.860 --> 00:20:49.500
and tweak each segment
to get the telescope

00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:51.393
to align on the star.

00:20:52.290 --> 00:20:53.730
Each mirror had to be aligned

00:20:53.730 --> 00:20:55.680
to within the billionth of a meter,

00:20:55.680 --> 00:20:57.753
to form a single, primary mirror.

00:20:58.710 --> 00:21:01.410
That required making ultra
fine adjustments equal

00:21:01.410 --> 00:21:04.680
to 1/10,000th the width of a human hair,

00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:07.083
which took roughly three
months to complete.

00:21:08.130 --> 00:21:11.400
So once that was done, we
got back this beautiful image

00:21:11.400 --> 00:21:15.270
of what's otherwise a
boring star that showed,

00:21:15.270 --> 00:21:19.080
that proved that the
telescope was aligned.

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:20.700
For me, one of the most exciting things

00:21:20.700 --> 00:21:23.430
about this boring star image is the fact

00:21:23.430 --> 00:21:25.740
that in this relatively short exposure,

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:28.350
you could see galaxies in the background.

00:21:28.350 --> 00:21:30.480
And so it gave us a little hint

00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:32.673
of what's to come with this telescope.

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:36.690
The star calibration image gave us

00:21:36.690 --> 00:21:39.393
the first glimpse of JWST's power.

00:21:41.100 --> 00:21:43.290
It will give scientists
the ability to peer

00:21:43.290 --> 00:21:45.300
into star-forming regions

00:21:45.300 --> 00:21:48.483
and to witness their birth
more clearly than ever before.

00:21:59.370 --> 00:22:00.960
We'll be looking back in time

00:22:00.960 --> 00:22:04.923
between 20,000 and 200,000 years.

00:22:09.360 --> 00:22:12.870
We will be looking at three
massive star forming regions.

00:22:12.870 --> 00:22:15.150
One is in the Milky Way, our galaxy

00:22:15.150 --> 00:22:18.453
and two are in nearby galaxies,
the Magellanic Clouds.

00:22:21.450 --> 00:22:25.980
NGC 3603 is a massive star
forming region located

00:22:25.980 --> 00:22:30.980
at about 20,000 light-years
away from us in the Milky Way.

00:22:31.020 --> 00:22:34.590
And it is made up of many
stars, many massive stars,

00:22:34.590 --> 00:22:36.960
some of which are even a hundred times

00:22:36.960 --> 00:22:38.580
more massive than the Sun,

00:22:38.580 --> 00:22:42.450
but there are many, many smaller
stars, stars like the Sun.

00:22:42.450 --> 00:22:44.550
The Tarantula Nebula is located

00:22:44.550 --> 00:22:46.110
in the Large Magellanic Cloud

00:22:46.110 --> 00:22:50.070
at a distance of about
150,000 light-years.

00:22:50.070 --> 00:22:53.220
The Tarantula is a massive
nursery of star formation.

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:56.250
There are millions of stars
there that were just born,

00:22:56.250 --> 00:22:59.073
and some of them are still
being created, being formed.

00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:04.950
And the third region that we
want to study is called NGC346,

00:23:04.950 --> 00:23:09.600
is farther away still
about 200,000 light-years

00:23:09.600 --> 00:23:12.603
in the Small Magellanic
Cloud, yet another galaxy.

00:23:14.940 --> 00:23:16.830
In each one of these regions,

00:23:16.830 --> 00:23:20.070
we will select about 100 stars

00:23:20.070 --> 00:23:21.870
and we will look at them very carefully

00:23:21.870 --> 00:23:23.400
by blocking everything else.

00:23:23.400 --> 00:23:25.230
We want to get only the light of those

00:23:25.230 --> 00:23:26.400
that we are interested.

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:29.730
So we can disperse the light
and produce a spectrum,

00:23:29.730 --> 00:23:31.080
very clear.

00:23:31.080 --> 00:23:35.610
But then with the camera, we
will look at the entire regions

00:23:35.610 --> 00:23:38.670
and they will be easily millions of stars

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:40.420
that we'll be seeing in these parts.

00:23:42.030 --> 00:23:44.100
We will be looking at the massive stars,

00:23:44.100 --> 00:23:47.340
but we are particularly
interested in stars like the Sun,

00:23:47.340 --> 00:23:49.320
because those are the most likely ones

00:23:49.320 --> 00:23:50.973
to have planets around them.

00:23:56.550 --> 00:24:00.060
The main goal is to understand
what is the difference

00:24:00.060 --> 00:24:04.170
in the way in which stars
form in our own galaxy,

00:24:04.170 --> 00:24:05.973
and also nearby galaxies.

00:24:07.590 --> 00:24:11.580
But unfortunately, visible
light doesn't travel well

00:24:11.580 --> 00:24:14.610
in regions where you
have lots of dust and gas

00:24:14.610 --> 00:24:18.900
and the regions where stars
are forming are full of dust.

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:23.490
So in those regions, it's very
difficult for visible light

00:24:23.490 --> 00:24:28.440
to go through but infrared
light has longer wavelength.

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:33.440
So the light rays are able
to go around those particles

00:24:33.750 --> 00:24:37.560
and we can see light even
when there is lots of dust.

00:24:37.560 --> 00:24:39.453
So we can see through the dust.

00:24:46.440 --> 00:24:49.560
Stars are the building block of galaxies.

00:24:49.560 --> 00:24:53.160
They produce most of the power,
the light in the Universe,

00:24:53.160 --> 00:24:57.630
and they also make up the
elements, elements like carbon,

00:24:57.630 --> 00:25:00.570
oxygen, nitrogen, iron.

00:25:00.570 --> 00:25:03.570
Most of the elements in our
body were actually made up

00:25:03.570 --> 00:25:04.530
in a star.

00:25:04.530 --> 00:25:06.210
And in fact, there is more,

00:25:06.210 --> 00:25:08.880
some of the heavy elements in our body

00:25:08.880 --> 00:25:13.880
like gold, copper, zinc actually formed

00:25:14.220 --> 00:25:17.550
when a massive star exploded as supernova.

00:25:17.550 --> 00:25:22.170
So even though studying how
stars form will not tell us

00:25:22.170 --> 00:25:23.970
who we are as human beings,

00:25:23.970 --> 00:25:26.280
it will definitely make us understand,

00:25:26.280 --> 00:25:29.400
make us realize that without stars,

00:25:29.400 --> 00:25:31.800
we would've never been
there in the first place.

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:38.030
But it's not all about stars and galaxies.

00:25:39.390 --> 00:25:42.360
JWST was also built with a relatively new,

00:25:42.360 --> 00:25:45.633
exciting field in mind, exoplanets.

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:49.503
Planets that are outside
of our own solar system.

00:25:51.390 --> 00:25:53.700
Although there weren't
any confirmed exoplanets

00:25:53.700 --> 00:25:56.070
while Hubble was being designed and built,

00:25:56.070 --> 00:25:59.670
it has contributed significantly
to this emerging field,

00:25:59.670 --> 00:26:02.583
giving us this first
visible-light image of one.

00:26:03.900 --> 00:26:07.680
Now there are over 5,000
confirmed exoplanets

00:26:07.680 --> 00:26:09.930
and in the last few decades, we've learned

00:26:09.930 --> 00:26:13.473
that the overwhelming majority
of stars have planets.

00:26:14.580 --> 00:26:18.240
JWST has capability to
study exoplanet atmospheres

00:26:18.240 --> 00:26:22.020
to a degree and precision that
has never been done before.

00:26:22.020 --> 00:26:24.090
And so the infrared part of the spectrum

00:26:24.090 --> 00:26:26.610
offers a really great insight

00:26:26.610 --> 00:26:28.980
into some of the really
interesting chemicals

00:26:28.980 --> 00:26:30.840
that might be in a planet's atmosphere,

00:26:30.840 --> 00:26:34.890
things like water vapor and
carbon dioxide and methane,

00:26:34.890 --> 00:26:38.220
things that could
possibly signal a surface

00:26:38.220 --> 00:26:39.930
that is habitable.

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:41.910
And there's one particular system

00:26:41.910 --> 00:26:43.770
that's on everyone's mind.

00:26:43.770 --> 00:26:47.400
It's called Trappist-1,
and it has multiple planets

00:26:47.400 --> 00:26:51.180
in the habitable zone, the
orbital region around a star

00:26:51.180 --> 00:26:53.550
in which an Earth-like
planet can have liquid water

00:26:53.550 --> 00:26:56.193
on its surface and possibly support life.

00:27:13.434 --> 00:27:15.780
We will be looking about
40 years back in time,

00:27:15.780 --> 00:27:17.673
which is literally in our backyard.

00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:22.380
The Trappist-1 system is
what we can call the rockstar

00:27:22.380 --> 00:27:23.213
of exoplanets.

00:27:23.213 --> 00:27:27.840
This is a star is very small,
not much bigger than Jupiter,

00:27:27.840 --> 00:27:29.790
and that star has not one,

00:27:29.790 --> 00:27:33.000
but seven planet transiting its star.

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:36.600
And we know that these
planets are of mass and sizes

00:27:36.600 --> 00:27:38.490
about similar to the
Earth and three of them

00:27:38.490 --> 00:27:41.160
and maybe four are right
in the habitable zone.

00:27:41.160 --> 00:27:42.990
And this system is very
close to the Earth,

00:27:42.990 --> 00:27:44.610
40 light-years away.

00:27:44.610 --> 00:27:47.580
And this is the one that we'll
be looking very hard to find

00:27:47.580 --> 00:27:49.260
whether these planets have an atmosphere.

00:27:49.260 --> 00:27:52.830
And if there's a system to
point where there's maybe life

00:27:52.830 --> 00:27:54.730
that's the one, the Trappist-1 system.

00:27:58.176 --> 00:28:00.690
The Hubble space telescopes
tried to detect the atmospheres

00:28:00.690 --> 00:28:02.340
and so far has failed.

00:28:02.340 --> 00:28:04.110
And we need a very powerful telescope

00:28:04.110 --> 00:28:04.943
like James Webb to do this.

00:28:04.943 --> 00:28:05.880
To detect their atmosphere,

00:28:05.880 --> 00:28:09.000
finding whether they have oxygen, water,

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:11.430
all the molecules that
are required for life

00:28:11.430 --> 00:28:12.430
as we know on earth.

00:28:15.180 --> 00:28:16.260
There are several methods,

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:18.090
but the one that will be used very much

00:28:18.090 --> 00:28:20.400
by James Webb is the transit method.

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:22.530
This is by chance you have a planet

00:28:22.530 --> 00:28:25.680
that goes in front of its
star from our perspective.

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:27.930
And you can see a little light dipping,

00:28:27.930 --> 00:28:30.270
as the planet goes in front of the star.

00:28:30.270 --> 00:28:34.020
And that allows us to measure
the radius of the planet.

00:28:34.020 --> 00:28:36.330
But also we can actually
probe the atmosphere.

00:28:36.330 --> 00:28:38.460
So the light that is
filtered by the stars,

00:28:38.460 --> 00:28:41.910
by measuring the colors of
the atmospheres gives us a lot

00:28:41.910 --> 00:28:43.980
of information about the atmosphere.

00:28:43.980 --> 00:28:46.410
It's called transit spectroscopy.

00:28:46.410 --> 00:28:49.229
And that's a technique
that's been used with Hubble,

00:28:49.229 --> 00:28:51.480
but with some limited performance

00:28:51.480 --> 00:28:53.220
and the James Webb Space Telescope

00:28:53.220 --> 00:28:54.690
will be very well optimized

00:28:54.690 --> 00:28:57.090
to do these kinds of very
delicate observations.

00:29:01.110 --> 00:29:03.720
The Trappist-1 system is
by far the best system.

00:29:03.720 --> 00:29:06.720
This is the one that will
give us the strongest signal.

00:29:06.720 --> 00:29:10.170
And there will be lots of
time devoted to that system

00:29:10.170 --> 00:29:13.230
with all science instruments onboard JWST.

00:29:13.230 --> 00:29:15.600
It is to do with the fact
that the star is very close.

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:18.060
So there's lots of light we
can get from the telescope.

00:29:18.060 --> 00:29:19.790
And also the relative size of the planet

00:29:19.790 --> 00:29:21.780
to the star is relatively large,

00:29:21.780 --> 00:29:23.550
and that makes things much easier.

00:29:23.550 --> 00:29:27.900
We love small star and big
planet and for Earth-like planet.

00:29:27.900 --> 00:29:30.873
Well, the Trappist-1 system
is just a perfect system.

00:29:34.080 --> 00:29:37.890
I think just knowing that these
planets have an atmosphere

00:29:37.890 --> 00:29:40.920
and knowing that we have a
planet in the habitable zone

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:44.640
and there's water on it, that
would be a big, big deal.

00:29:44.640 --> 00:29:46.980
That would be a first step
to say, look, this is here.

00:29:46.980 --> 00:29:48.530
We need to look in that system.

00:29:52.080 --> 00:29:54.180
Dr. Doyon was among the scientists

00:29:54.180 --> 00:29:57.420
who worked on the telescope
as principal investigator

00:29:57.420 --> 00:30:00.723
of the instrument provided
by the Canadian Space Agency.

00:30:03.870 --> 00:30:06.300
The telescope was such
a massive undertaking

00:30:06.300 --> 00:30:09.390
with numerous science goals
and technical challenges,

00:30:09.390 --> 00:30:11.880
that it required the
collaboration of NASA,

00:30:11.880 --> 00:30:15.663
the Canadian Space Agency,
and the European Space Agency.

00:30:18.030 --> 00:30:19.710
So with the James Webb Space Telescope,

00:30:19.710 --> 00:30:22.440
which astronomers started
thinking about as early

00:30:22.440 --> 00:30:25.800
as the end of the '80s, even
before Hubble was launched,

00:30:25.800 --> 00:30:27.900
European astronomers were involved along

00:30:27.900 --> 00:30:31.050
with our American colleagues
and with the Canadians,

00:30:31.050 --> 00:30:33.030
from the beginning, from the outset,

00:30:33.030 --> 00:30:36.000
and built a bigger and better telescope

00:30:36.000 --> 00:30:38.650
that wouldn't be possible
with just one agency alone.

00:30:40.170 --> 00:30:42.150
Scientists who worked on the telescope

00:30:42.150 --> 00:30:45.300
were allotted time but
anyone could submit proposals

00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:46.713
from anywhere in the world.

00:30:49.620 --> 00:30:51.510
This whole process is dual anonymous.

00:30:51.510 --> 00:30:55.440
So don't know who's proposing
and who's doing the grading.

00:30:55.440 --> 00:30:58.563
And that's the way that the
telescope time is allocated.

00:30:59.490 --> 00:31:00.390
One of the great things

00:31:00.390 --> 00:31:02.940
about these NASA astrophysics missions

00:31:02.940 --> 00:31:06.600
is that anyone in the world
with a good idea can apply

00:31:06.600 --> 00:31:08.370
to use time on the telescope.

00:31:08.370 --> 00:31:11.670
And that happens, we get
proposals from around the world,

00:31:11.670 --> 00:31:14.010
to use the telescope and
at the end of the day,

00:31:14.010 --> 00:31:15.813
the best proposals are chosen.

00:31:18.060 --> 00:31:20.610
Anonymous proposals
meant that bias was kept

00:31:20.610 --> 00:31:24.690
to a minimum, and ensured that
JWST covered a broad range

00:31:24.690 --> 00:31:27.450
of science goals, even ones focusing

00:31:27.450 --> 00:31:29.073
on our own solar system.

00:31:42.360 --> 00:31:45.720
We are looking at the farthest regions

00:31:45.720 --> 00:31:47.070
of our solar system.

00:31:47.070 --> 00:31:50.610
So this is 4.6 hours that
the light takes to come

00:31:50.610 --> 00:31:51.443
to the Earth.

00:31:53.070 --> 00:31:56.340
The acronym trans-Neptunian
objects is TNOs.

00:31:56.340 --> 00:31:57.810
These are objects that are orbiting

00:31:57.810 --> 00:31:59.490
beyond the orbit of Neptune

00:31:59.490 --> 00:32:03.000
so in the coldest regions
of our solar system.

00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:05.130
There are estimated to be billions

00:32:05.130 --> 00:32:08.220
of trans-Neptunian objects,
even if at this moment,

00:32:08.220 --> 00:32:11.163
we have only discovered
around 3000 of them.

00:32:14.313 --> 00:32:17.850
TNOs are one of the most pristine bodies

00:32:17.850 --> 00:32:19.080
in our solar system.

00:32:19.080 --> 00:32:23.730
That means that they have
been not too much processed

00:32:23.730 --> 00:32:25.590
since they formed.

00:32:25.590 --> 00:32:28.830
So they hold clues and
they hold information

00:32:28.830 --> 00:32:30.900
from the very first
stages of the formation

00:32:30.900 --> 00:32:32.730
of the solar system.

00:32:32.730 --> 00:32:36.090
This basically means that they
are like frozen time capsules

00:32:36.090 --> 00:32:39.513
that are there waiting for
us to unveil those secrets.

00:32:41.070 --> 00:32:44.700
The largest objects in the
trans-Neptunian Belt together

00:32:44.700 --> 00:32:48.270
with Pluto is Haumea, Eris and Makemake,

00:32:48.270 --> 00:32:51.720
and because they are so
large, they have activity,

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:54.930
they may have an atmosphere,
they have a lot of ice

00:32:54.930 --> 00:32:58.590
like methane, nitrogen, CO.

00:32:58.590 --> 00:33:00.960
Those are in the largest TNOs

00:33:00.960 --> 00:33:03.180
and we think they were an ingredient

00:33:03.180 --> 00:33:05.310
for the formation of all the TNOs.

00:33:05.310 --> 00:33:08.460
But when it comes to really
know what is on the surface

00:33:08.460 --> 00:33:10.743
of these objects, we don't really know.

00:33:13.470 --> 00:33:16.440
So we want to look if in the smaller TNOs,

00:33:16.440 --> 00:33:19.920
there are also some kind,
some amounts of these ice.

00:33:19.920 --> 00:33:21.990
We want to know which are the ingredients

00:33:21.990 --> 00:33:24.960
that are on the surface,
and which is the state,

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:28.500
the physical and chemical
state of these ingredients,

00:33:28.500 --> 00:33:30.990
the recipe to cook a small body.

00:33:30.990 --> 00:33:33.300
We have 60 TNOs in our sample,

00:33:33.300 --> 00:33:35.940
we have representation of all the colors.

00:33:35.940 --> 00:33:38.520
What we are doing is, we take the light

00:33:38.520 --> 00:33:42.240
that comes from one of these
bodies and then it's channeled

00:33:42.240 --> 00:33:44.970
through this instrument is called NIRSpec,

00:33:44.970 --> 00:33:47.610
and this is a near infrared spectrograph.

00:33:47.610 --> 00:33:50.910
NIRSpec is going to serve
in a part of the light

00:33:50.910 --> 00:33:54.420
that can't be reached
for TNOs from the earth.

00:33:54.420 --> 00:33:59.420
So we are just separating the
light to get to know really,

00:34:00.240 --> 00:34:02.580
each single material that can be there.

00:34:02.580 --> 00:34:05.160
So James Webb comes on time

00:34:05.160 --> 00:34:07.560
and is perfect to do this science

00:34:07.560 --> 00:34:09.453
because it's out of the atmosphere.

00:34:13.320 --> 00:34:16.050
We have a preconceived idea

00:34:16.050 --> 00:34:19.800
of what is a TNO based on
the knowledge that we have.

00:34:19.800 --> 00:34:23.730
But our knowledge is limited
and the best things about tools

00:34:23.730 --> 00:34:28.080
like JWST is that they are
really discovery tools.

00:34:28.080 --> 00:34:32.310
They are going to provide
us with many answers

00:34:32.310 --> 00:34:36.123
to questions that we
didn't even know we had.

00:34:40.740 --> 00:34:42.690
Apart from JWST,

00:34:42.690 --> 00:34:45.240
many ground-based telescopes
will be coming online

00:34:45.240 --> 00:34:46.710
in the next few years,

00:34:46.710 --> 00:34:49.533
complimenting the science JWST will do.

00:34:52.440 --> 00:34:55.260
Like the Vera Rubin Observatory in Chile,

00:34:55.260 --> 00:34:57.963
estimated to come online in 2023.

00:34:59.130 --> 00:35:01.410
It is a wide survey telescope expecting

00:35:01.410 --> 00:35:04.830
to discover thousands of
new objects like TNOs,

00:35:04.830 --> 00:35:08.220
so then telescopes like
JWST can look at them

00:35:08.220 --> 00:35:09.303
in more detail.

00:35:10.290 --> 00:35:13.683
And the next big space telescope
is just around the corner.

00:35:14.520 --> 00:35:18.600
The big telescopes like Hubble
and JWST only come along,

00:35:18.600 --> 00:35:20.190
once every couple of decades.

00:35:20.190 --> 00:35:23.310
And the next big telescope
like this, that we're building

00:35:23.310 --> 00:35:26.130
is the Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope.

00:35:26.130 --> 00:35:28.890
So that particular telescope
is not quite as big

00:35:28.890 --> 00:35:33.300
or expensive as JWST, but it
is also an infrared telescope

00:35:33.300 --> 00:35:34.920
that's designed to see the Universe

00:35:34.920 --> 00:35:37.170
in more of a broad swath.

00:35:37.170 --> 00:35:40.590
So telescopes like Hubble
and JWST are designed

00:35:40.590 --> 00:35:43.560
to see small parts of
the Universe very deeply,

00:35:43.560 --> 00:35:46.530
but by looking a little
less deep, but more wide,

00:35:46.530 --> 00:35:48.980
we can learn different
things about the Universe.

00:35:53.490 --> 00:35:56.040
People often ask, are
these telescopes worth it?

00:35:56.040 --> 00:35:58.620
We're spending a lot of
money on these telescopes.

00:35:58.620 --> 00:36:01.200
JWST is $10 billion.

00:36:01.200 --> 00:36:04.530
Hubble from its late
inception in the late '70s

00:36:04.530 --> 00:36:07.260
all the way to now is about $16 billion

00:36:07.260 --> 00:36:10.650
and so the question of is it
worth it is an important one.

00:36:10.650 --> 00:36:12.300
These are funded by taxpayers

00:36:12.300 --> 00:36:14.010
and of course all of that money

00:36:14.010 --> 00:36:16.440
that's quote, unquote sent
into space is spent right here

00:36:16.440 --> 00:36:19.560
on Earth, of course,
funding high tech jobs.

00:36:19.560 --> 00:36:21.060
But in addition to that,

00:36:21.060 --> 00:36:24.390
I think if we look at the
past 30 years of Hubble

00:36:24.390 --> 00:36:28.950
and what we foresee as a
bright future with JWST,

00:36:28.950 --> 00:36:33.060
if you put that in the context
of money spent per year,

00:36:33.060 --> 00:36:36.090
it turns out to be less
than a cup of coffee,

00:36:36.090 --> 00:36:39.483
a cheap cup of coffee,
per citizen, per year.

00:36:48.270 --> 00:36:50.306
Human beings are curious.

00:36:50.306 --> 00:36:52.913
We all want to know from where we come.

00:36:52.913 --> 00:36:56.700
Yes, we exist because stars exist.

00:36:56.700 --> 00:37:01.140
And so it's good for us to
understand what are the elements

00:37:01.140 --> 00:37:04.020
and processes that are
actually needed for us

00:37:04.020 --> 00:37:06.870
as human beings to exist.

00:37:06.870 --> 00:37:09.300
I know for myself, thinking
over the past year,

00:37:09.300 --> 00:37:12.900
the past couple of years with
so much going on in the world

00:37:12.900 --> 00:37:16.950
and so much negativity
having the positivity

00:37:16.950 --> 00:37:19.530
of a mission like this to look forward to

00:37:19.530 --> 00:37:22.140
and something that's
working and successful

00:37:22.140 --> 00:37:25.020
and is a symbol of people
all over the world,

00:37:25.020 --> 00:37:27.690
working together to achieve something

00:37:27.690 --> 00:37:30.060
that is technologically amazing.

00:37:30.060 --> 00:37:31.830
For me that's awe-inspiring

00:37:31.830 --> 00:37:34.920
and gives me hope for
the future of humanity.

00:37:34.920 --> 00:37:37.020
It's so easy to get wrapped up in things

00:37:37.020 --> 00:37:39.000
that are happening on planet Earth

00:37:39.000 --> 00:37:41.523
and not go and think about
these beautiful things

00:37:41.523 --> 00:37:42.510
that we see.

00:37:42.510 --> 00:37:45.390
But then on the other hand,
things like understanding

00:37:45.390 --> 00:37:48.720
how stars work can teach
us about nuclear fusion,

00:37:48.720 --> 00:37:52.530
for example, the way that
the elements are produced

00:37:52.530 --> 00:37:53.610
in the Universe.

00:37:53.610 --> 00:37:55.170
I think it's just a fundamental question

00:37:55.170 --> 00:37:57.450
that human beings should want to know.

00:37:57.450 --> 00:38:01.770
Historically, whenever a new
facility is put together,

00:38:01.770 --> 00:38:03.780
and you ask the question
five, 10 years later,

00:38:03.780 --> 00:38:06.390
what was the main discovery
that this telescope made?

00:38:06.390 --> 00:38:07.423
Nobody could predict it.

00:38:07.423 --> 00:38:09.450
I can talk to you about all
the kind of stuff we think

00:38:09.450 --> 00:38:11.010
we're gonna be doing, but you know what,

00:38:11.010 --> 00:38:13.890
we don't know, and that's the
most exciting part of this.

00:38:13.890 --> 00:38:17.520
Who has not wondered
about looking at the sky?

00:38:17.520 --> 00:38:21.600
Who does not wonder about
knowing that we are not alone?

00:38:21.600 --> 00:38:24.480
All of that knowledge that wonders us,

00:38:24.480 --> 00:38:27.723
it comes because someone asked himself,

00:38:28.650 --> 00:38:29.723
what is that that is out there?

